#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151872 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "a18b37e2-4ab1-5fd1-b985-3021172abc41" :question/math-academy-id "e-6306" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2} \\, \\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y = x$ for $\\, 0 \\le x \\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y = y(t) = t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{{t}^{2}}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}({4}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151873 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e381c74c-43ed-5588-ba9a-4c7d4fb2caf3" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "b4f26e02-fb0f-5577-90f4-52e7eeabd857" :question/math-academy-id "e-6310" :question/problem "Consider the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^2}{x} \\,\\textrm ds,\n$$\n\n where $C$ is the section of the curve $y = \\dfrac{x^3}3$ between the points $(0,0)$ and $(3,9).$\nWrite the line integral in the form \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y = y(t) = \\dfrac{t^3}{3}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{t^3}3 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 3,\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3})j \\\\  & =i+{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^2}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3})}^{2}}{t}=\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{5}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e381c74c-43ed-5588-ba9a-4c7d4fb2caf3" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c62ed926-d2c6-53f5-aef8-3ea86c727387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332853} {:answer/id #uuid "002f2f6a-cece-5aee-a1b8-db89629437bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332854} {:answer/id #uuid "96e5d311-52a9-5f8f-a56e-6e51d5e43454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332855} {:answer/id #uuid "48b60cf1-51ea-53ac-8f44-e698d48e2068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332856} {:answer/id #uuid "9edd5d6f-de73-536a-ae95-f0c28c6892e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "002f2f6a-cece-5aee-a1b8-db89629437bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332854} :answer-field/id #uuid "36f1a1e4-0759-599c-93ca-97425a07f999" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d45e7151-1e23-5fc3-b354-b72fb9448af9" :question/math-academy-id "q-148205" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=2{e}^{x}$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2{e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+2{e}^{t}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2{e}^{t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e381c74c-43ed-5588-ba9a-4c7d4fb2caf3" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c00917ce-2a5c-5829-a679-2ce288a5dc75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{1+9{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332860} {:answer/id #uuid "08e4f50a-d9a5-5883-b373-e47f9fc9007c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332861} {:answer/id #uuid "2b707ba3-49ba-57dc-bfd9-41d660281ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332862} {:answer/id #uuid "a79ac071-240f-5c3f-a23c-0b549a450f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+9{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332863} {:answer/id #uuid "1b0d7911-d9c3-575d-a17e-8f2ac9b5aa29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{9+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c00917ce-2a5c-5829-a679-2ce288a5dc75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{1+9{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332860} :answer-field/id #uuid "94887495-1825-5caf-bdac-fcbf5ea3a5e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a59998c8-cbbc-5d7a-af75-6c34d38fd2f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-148206" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y={x}^{3}$ between the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,8)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={t}^{3}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})j \\\\  & =i+3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+9{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t\\cdot {t}^{3}={t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{1+9{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e381c74c-43ed-5588-ba9a-4c7d4fb2caf3" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f722b16-f135-528a-bd12-b1275d894549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332867} {:answer/id #uuid "7d49caf1-de0d-5934-9045-65e5c694a515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332868} {:answer/id #uuid "89d1c7bc-9fd3-5b6c-a6c5-de3691333958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332869} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ea6ebd-d51a-593e-8221-fc80100fc3d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332870} {:answer/id #uuid "23a9f3e7-454d-5f29-8f6d-10440c9ac9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89d1c7bc-9fd3-5b6c-a6c5-de3691333958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332869} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c7cedc-85af-581e-b1fd-0eeff7a3a113" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "654b0531-93fc-5dce-886a-dd09b8c9b7d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-148208" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}2{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ between the points $(1,1)$ and $(2,\\frac{1}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{1}{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\frac{1}{t}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\cdot {t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}=2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb82b69d-e498-5a0e-ac00-bf22605f30bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332874} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed1d504-b535-58ed-94db-3cefffaabd88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332875} {:answer/id #uuid "348e88bb-2c76-5e7a-947c-84e522c11bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332876} {:answer/id #uuid "6e27ee1a-9618-51f0-9b54-7832b517a268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332877} {:answer/id #uuid "2f16eab3-b665-5415-9b3e-f6cef248802e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "348e88bb-2c76-5e7a-947c-84e522c11bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332876} :answer-field/id #uuid "72f769f8-8851-50fe-a4b3-9600e330abc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b07150e-77a5-52c9-8482-524fcb3a104e" :question/math-academy-id "q-148215" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2xy$ along the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2t\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2t\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2({2}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c242ced6-a2ca-5920-b983-e70af5991abd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332881} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c7f2aa-249a-5ffa-99f1-f7ef5595e81b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23\\sqrt{10}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332882} {:answer/id #uuid "39130b47-18c6-5f87-9d8f-9f5fbaafd00c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332883} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4371d1-9556-5ab6-8213-e5b5ae98af1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332884} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf2028c-d405-54a3-a4f7-e81aedd99caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8c7f2aa-249a-5ffa-99f1-f7ef5595e81b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23\\sqrt{10}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332882} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c8d70cc-1dcf-512d-9100-ccabc6e29dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d50e2dc2-5a95-5b5e-8e6e-dc579dcef141" :question/math-academy-id "q-148218" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)$ along the line $y=3x+2,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,3t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)j \\\\  & =i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{2}+(3t+2)={t}^{2}+3t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\,(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+2t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{2}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{23\\sqrt{10}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "865752c5-0643-501a-a344-18ff9d59e8e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332888} {:answer/id #uuid "dba1eb6e-0b30-5b2b-8269-379ac7725003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332889} {:answer/id #uuid "61855633-cab7-524d-afd5-870007d0dbfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332890} {:answer/id #uuid "df993077-9bb7-57e4-ad21-d81845b8a2d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332891} {:answer/id #uuid "8610620a-efaf-512b-b791-0acbbb7d74fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8610620a-efaf-512b-b791-0acbbb7d74fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332892} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5ccb37a-b796-5fa5-a990-aaae64be7237" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee1f1941-034e-5d98-aef0-26601af4ff84" :question/math-academy-id "q-148219" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-y}$ along the line $y=x-4,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=t-4.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4)j \\\\  & =i+\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(t-4))}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\,t\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e381c74c-43ed-5588-ba9a-4c7d4fb2caf3" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37854e47-43c1-59db-9839-77b92866a71b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+2){e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332895} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc34625-6300-5379-9d0f-0b41fce78c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332896} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5ad67b-0fe6-5535-9f8c-c298fdf074e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{5t+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332897} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4491ec-4912-5ee9-b2bb-781d349788b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\sqrt{26t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332898} {:answer/id #uuid "6db0540b-7378-5e8e-a50c-a26025d1cd4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db0540b-7378-5e8e-a50c-a26025d1cd4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332899} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0c88b3b-e86f-568b-b9fa-151498221930" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e9178a1-252d-5550-bc1e-b9c399e6cd7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-76414" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=5x+2$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=5t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,5t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+2)j \\\\  & =i+5\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(5t+2){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{26}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e381c74c-43ed-5588-ba9a-4c7d4fb2caf3" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ece546b-8e3c-553d-a159-1d2c9db04092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332902} {:answer/id #uuid "7e188389-d96a-54df-8719-e1ffd110911e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332903} {:answer/id #uuid "05c9dc56-7899-5da3-a5bc-cbc18e4baa1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332904} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d50d5f-7ede-520b-8c99-95e3959aed19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332905} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4236a2-dd17-5863-bd5d-799fce8d0143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05c9dc56-7899-5da3-a5bc-cbc18e4baa1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332904} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c76bd77-1257-59ec-85e3-ec8a622dc8ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99fdd141-25ed-55d8-bec8-14b1e001a397" :question/math-academy-id "q-76416" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}2{x}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ for $\\,1\\le x\\le 2e?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t).$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2e\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t))j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{e}^{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2e}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2e}2{t}^{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5c3457-e383-5443-974a-ee7b2309802f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332909} {:answer/id #uuid "71ddd68d-c834-5fc5-a1da-bbc9609c85da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332910} {:answer/id #uuid "77376dbe-3a38-5195-bfc3-51cf1d120b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332911} {:answer/id #uuid "92fda09c-dc66-5179-ab9b-5db5ef4026cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332912} {:answer/id #uuid "144b9029-f37d-5fbc-908d-1c6451f3fbae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71ddd68d-c834-5fc5-a1da-bbc9609c85da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332910} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ac526d-ec36-58a3-a158-23749532ce78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a40fb3b-6569-51c0-abf6-2ad641c651e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-76431" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4\\sqrt{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 4\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{t}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})}{t}=\\frac{t}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{t}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{25}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3}{|}_{1}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({25}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(125-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df476a83-9a43-559a-a22e-d5e316d0e798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(5\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150907} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0809e4-5085-51c7-b1bc-a3ecc86ac5a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150908} {:answer/id #uuid "2947ac70-dd53-5663-a131-2e6877056f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(5\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150909} {:answer/id #uuid "5593042e-6da9-5e30-9c80-86791741e5f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150910} {:answer/id #uuid "79eb5ab5-ab77-5426-b900-782d708aa5fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd0809e4-5085-51c7-b1bc-a3ecc86ac5a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150908} :answer-field/id #uuid "094256cf-51df-5726-9725-6493c1dfd31e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78a90830-8528-5d8e-9b99-56c7720a064a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76483" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$ along the curve $y={e}^{x},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({10}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(10\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
